<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Schellenberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1317 CE – 1719 CE (approx.) · capital Schellenberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Rhine-valley lordship that the princes of Liechtenstein bought in 1699 and joined with Vaduz to create their principality.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lordship took its name from the Schellenberg family, Bavarian ministeriales who settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> valley in the 13th century and built castles on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eschnerberg">Eschnerberg</a> ridge. After they sold their rights in the early 14th century, the lordship passed to other houses, among them the barons of Brandis, the counts of Sulz and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-hohenems">County of Hohenems</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Schellenberg was a rural district of a few villages, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eschen">Eschen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauren">Mauren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamprin">Gamprin</a>, ruled by distant lords through a bailiff. Its peasants lived from farming and livestock and kept local assemblies. It was usually held together with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-vaduz">County of Vaduz</a>, and both were direct fiefs of the empire, a status that later proved valuable.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The heavily indebted counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-hohenems">Hohenems</a> sold Schellenberg in 1699 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-adam-i-prince-of-liechtenstein">Prince Johann Adam Andreas of Liechtenstein</a>, who wanted an imperial territory to win a seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. In 1719 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Charles VI</a> united Schellenberg and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-vaduz">Vaduz</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein</a>, where it survives as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unterland-liechtenstein">Unterland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-schellenberg.jpg" alt="Lordship of Schellenberg" width="320" height="193"><figcaption>Image: arz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Liechtenstein_(unknown-1719)_1.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1317 CE – 1719 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Schellenberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Schellenberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1518&amp;focus=lordship-of-schellenberg" aria-label="Lordship of Schellenberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4904 520 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Schellenberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5224 776L5229 694M5224 776L5223 779"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5223 779h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 776h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 776h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1518&amp;focus=lordship-of-schellenberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
